     1  # Report: Four-Hour Reproducibility Pilot
     2  ## How Input Order Affects Runtime of sorted() vs Insertion Sort
     3  
     4  ---
     5  
     6  ### 1. Question
     7  
     8  How does input order affect the runtime of Python's built-in `sorted()` compared
     9  with a correct pure-Python insertion sort for small integer lists?
    10  
    11  ### 2. Hypothesis
    12  
    13  Python's `sorted()` uses Timsort, which is O(n log n) worst-case but adaptive
    14  to presorted data (O(n) best case). A correct insertion sort is O(n²) worst-case
    15  but O(n) best case for already-sorted inputs. We hypothesize:
    16  
    17  - Both algorithms will be fastest on sorted input and slowest on reverse-sorted.
    18  - The ratio (insertion_sort time / sorted() time) will be smallest for sorted
    19    input and largest for reverse-sorted input.
    20  - The ratio will grow with list size, especially for reverse-sorted input,
    21    confirming the O(n²) vs O(n log n) complexity difference.
    22  
    23  ### 3. Methodology
    24  
    25  #### 3.1 Algorithms
    26  
    27  - **builtin_sorted**: Python's built-in `sorted()` function (Timsort, C-implemented).
    28  - **insertion_sort**: Pure-Python insertion sort (O(n²) worst case, O(n) best case).
    29  
    30  #### 3.2 Input Generation
    31  
    32  Four input types at three sizes (32, 128, 512):
    33  
    34  | Input Type    | Description                                    | Seed |
    35  |---------------|------------------------------------------------|------|
    36  | random        | Random integers in [-1000, 1000]               | 42   |
    37  | sorted        | Random integers, then sorted ascending         | 1    |
    38  | reverse       | Random integers, then sorted descending        | 1    |
    39  | duplicates    | Random integers in [0, 3] (only 4 distinct)    | 99   |
    40  
    41  All seeds are deterministic and recorded.
    42  
    43  #### 3.3 Timing
    44  
    45  - **Timer**: `time.perf_counter()` — highest-resolution monotonic clock available.
    46  - **Copying**: NOT timed. Each sort receives a fresh `list(data)` copy created
    47    BEFORE `time.perf_counter()` starts. This isolates the sort operation from
    48    allocation overhead.
    49  - **Repetitions**: 50 per condition (exceeds the 30 minimum).
    50  - **Randomization**: Measurement order across all 24 conditions × 2 algorithms
    51    was randomized with a fixed seed (12345) to reduce systematic bias from
    52    CPU frequency scaling, background processes, and thermal effects.
    53  
    54  #### 3.4 Correctness
    55  
    56  Every measured input was verified: `insertion_sort(data) == sorted(data)`.
    57  Additional edge-case tests: empty list, singleton, negatives only, mixed
    58  positive/negative, all duplicates, already sorted, reverse sorted.
    59  
    60  #### 3.5 Platform
    61  
    62  - Python 3.13.15, CPython
    63  - Linux 7.0.14-linuxkit-aarch64 (aarch64 / ARM64)
    64  - glibc 2.41
    65  
    66  ### 4. Results
    67  
    68  #### 4.1 Correctness Tests
    69  
    70  All 19 correctness tests passed (7 edge cases + 12 measured inputs × 1 algorithm).
    71  
    72  #### 4.2 Timing Summary (microseconds, mean ± 1σ)
    73  
    74  | Size | Input Type  | sorted()       | insertion_sort | Ratio (i/b) |
    75  |------|-------------|----------------|----------------|-------------|
    76  | 32   | random      | 0.37 ± 0.02    | 10.85 ± 0.97   | 29.5x       |
    77  | 32   | sorted      | 0.24 ± 0.09    | 1.54 ± 0.05    | 6.4x        |
    78  | 32   | reverse     | 0.26 ± 0.07    | 19.64 ± 0.58   | 75.3x       |
    79  | 32   | duplicates  | 0.32 ± 0.03    | 9.09 ± 1.97    | 28.0x       |
    80  | 128  | random      | 1.91 ± 0.68    | 140.41 ± 3.20  | 73.4x       |
    81  | 128  | sorted      | 0.55 ± 0.02    | 5.65 ± 0.14    | 10.2x       |
    82  | 128  | reverse     | 0.70 ± 0.13    | 299.51 ± 3.38  | 430.9x      |
    83  | 128  | duplicates  | 1.49 ± 0.16    | 109.40 ± 2.80  | 73.4x       |
    84  | 512  | random      | 10.00 ± 0.63   | 2787.53 ± 32.10| 278.6x      |
    85  | 512  | sorted      | 1.80 ± 0.09    | 27.54 ± 0.60   | 15.3x       |
    86  | 512  | reverse     | 2.34 ± 0.03    | 5220.90 ± 75.54| 2234.6x     |
    87  | 512  | duplicates  | 9.04 ± 2.33    | 2311.15 ± 231.27| 255.8x     |
    88  
    89  #### 4.3 Key Observations
    90  
    91  1. **Insertion sort is always slower** than `sorted()` across all conditions.
    92     The builtin is C-implemented and uses Timsort; the insertion sort is
    93     pure-Python with O(n²) worst-case complexity.
    94  
    95  2. **Input order matters dramatically for insertion sort**:
    96     - Sorted input: 6–15× slower (best case, O(n) comparisons)
    97     - Reverse sorted: 75–2235× slower (worst case, O(n²) comparisons)
    98     - The ratio grows superlinearly with size for reverse input, consistent
    99       with O(n²) complexity.
   100  
   101  3. **Timsort also benefits from presortedness**:
   102     - sorted() on sorted input is ~2× faster than on random input at each size.
   103     - This confirms Timsort's adaptive behavior.
   104  
   105  4. **Duplicate-heavy inputs** perform similarly to random inputs for insertion
   106     sort, suggesting the duplicate range (0–3) doesn't reduce comparisons enough
   107     to approach the O(n) best case.
   108  
   109  ### 5. Limitations
   110  
   111  - **Single platform**: Results are from one machine (aarch64 Linux). Timing
   112    values are absolute and will differ on other hardware.
   113  - **Relative comparisons are robust**: The ratio between algorithms is more
   114    meaningful than absolute times, and randomization of measurement order
   115    reduces systematic bias.
   116  - **50 repetitions ≠ 50 independent samples**: Measurements are taken in the
   117    same process under similar conditions. They reduce noise but do not
   118    constitute independent experimental units.
   119  - **Small sizes only**: Sizes 32, 128, 512 are small. At larger sizes,
   120    insertion sort would become impractical (O(n²) dominates).
   121  - **Integer-only inputs**: Results may differ for other data types (strings,
   122    floats, objects with custom comparison).
   123  - **No warm-up**: The Python interpreter and CPU may have been in different
   124    thermal/power states during the experiment.
   125  
   126  ### 6. Reproducibility
   127  
   128  ```bash
   129  cd /workspace/experiment_001
   130  python3 test_correctness.py    # Correctness tests
   131  python3 benchmark.py            # Run benchmark (writes measurements.csv)
   132  python3 verify_from_csv.py measurements.csv  # Verify from raw data
   133  ```
   134  
   135  Raw data: `measurements.csv` (1200 rows, ~89 KB)
   136  Scripts: `benchmark.py`, `test_correctness.py`, `verify_from_csv.py`
   137  
   138  ### 7. Conclusion
   139  
   140  Input order has a large effect on insertion sort runtime (6× to 2235× variation
   141  across conditions), while `sorted()` (Timsort) is more robust but still shows
   142  adaptive behavior (~2× faster on sorted vs random). The builtin is always faster
   143  in absolute terms due to C implementation and superior algorithm. The results
   144  are consistent with theoretical complexity analysis.


